WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.300
Secure ASP.NET Core engineering is a repeatable workflow.

2
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:20.600
It starts with understanding the application boundaries, continues through hosting configuration and middleware order, and shows up in everyday coding decisions: what to validate, where to authorize, how to handle errors, which data to return, and what to log.

3
00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.160
The pieces connect.

4
00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.300
Configuration choices affect middleware behavior.

5
00:00:25.400 --> 00:00:29.160
Middleware order affects identity and endpoint protection.

6
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:32.860
Model binding and validation affect data access.

7
00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:38.960
Authentication creates identity context, but authorization decides what that identity may do.

8
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:44.020
Browser defenses support the request model, but they do not replace server-side enforcement.

9
00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:52.520
Secrets and Data Protection influence how sessions, tokens, and sensitive payloads remain trustworthy across deployment environments.

10
00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:55.920
Security also continues after release.

11
00:00:56.020 --> 00:01:08.420
Logging, monitoring, dependency review, runtime maintenance, CI/CD protection, and deployment configuration determine whether the team can see problems, patch safely, and respond with confidence.

12
00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:15.720
A strong implementation with weak operations can still fail in production because the system cannot be maintained or investigated well.

13
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:19.440
The practical takeaway is to make security ordinary.

14
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:34.340
Use framework features intentionally, remove development-only behavior, prefer clear validation and authorization patterns, protect secrets, avoid custom cryptography, review high-risk workflows, and keep production feedback connected to engineering work.

15
00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:38.920
Small habits repeated across features are what reduce recurring risk.

16
00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.960
Secure ASP.NET Core applications are not built by memorizing every possible mistake.

17
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:56.980
They are built by teams that understand trust, make decisions explicit, use supported platform features, and keep improving the system as requirements, dependencies, and environments change.
